WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:03.470
It's time we look at some of the key functions

2
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.509
that the widget system exposes out to developers.

3
00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:08.109
Understanding these functions will set us up

4
00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:12.060
with a good knowledge base for us to build UI elements.

5
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.359
On the left, we've got screenshots

6
00:00:13.359 --> 00:00:15.820
of the blueprint versions of some of these key functions

7
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.519
whilst on the right, we've got screenshots

8
00:00:17.519 --> 00:00:21.620
of the code functions that we'd find inside user widget.

9
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.219
Firstly, we've got construct,

10
00:00:23.219 --> 00:00:24.980
and this will be called when the UI has been added

11
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:25.940
to the viewport.

12
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.019
And we can think of this as our begin play function

13
00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:29.260
that we'd usually see when working

14
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.270
with actors and components.

15
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:33.509
From there, we've got tick

16
00:00:33.509 --> 00:00:35.270
and this behaves exactly as you'd expect.

17
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.869
This function will be called regularly and consistently

18
00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:40.549
with each frame being the default.

19
00:00:40.549 --> 00:00:41.950
Finally, we've got destruct,

20
00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:43.850
and this will be called when the UI is removed

21
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.109
from the viewport or the parent.

22
00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:48.189
We can think of this as our end play function

23
00:00:48.189 --> 00:00:49.030
that we'd usually see

24
00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.380
when working with actors or components.

25
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.560
Moving on, we've got preconstruct.

26
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:55.539
This function will be called

27
00:00:55.539 --> 00:00:58.100
as our widget is created the same as construct.

28
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.820
However, the function will be called

29
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.280
in both the game and editor.

30
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.679
The purpose of this function is purely

31
00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:06.739
for cosmetic updates that use locally owned data,

32
00:01:06.739 --> 00:01:10.799
so you can't safely access the game at this point.

33
00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:14.230
Lastly, we've got mouse button down and mouse button up.

34
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.189
These functions are called from the widget system

35
00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:18.349
to notify that the mouse button has been pressed

36
00:01:18.349 --> 00:01:21.030
or released within the bounds of this widget.

37
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.310
And we typically use these functions

38
00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.430
to drive drag and drop functionality.
